<article class="page prose-page">
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>What a check represents</h1>
    <p class="lead">Miżien weighs public statements against the evidence. This page explains what its verdicts, drafts,
      data and maps mean, and what they do not.</p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-statement">
    <h2 id="h-statement">A verdict is about a statement</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A verdict applies to <strong>one statement, as the speaker worded it, on the date it was made</strong>. It
          is assessed against the evidence available when the check's evidence was last reviewed; each check shows
          that date.</li>
      <li>It is <strong>not a judgement of a person</strong>, an organisation, a party or a government as a whole,
          and not a judgement of anyone's motives or good faith. Nothing on this site says anyone acted in bad faith
          unless documents show it.</li>
      <li><strong>Not substantiated does not mean false.</strong> It means the evidence offered or available does not
          show the claim as stated. It may be true under some conditions.</li>
      <li>Misleading and Contradicted are given only on documents or data that can be shown, and each such check lists
          them.</li>
      <li>A check is not legal advice and not the finding of a court or regulator. Where a matter is before a court or
          tribunal, checks keep to the science and the public record.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-stages">
    <h2 id="h-stages">Drafts, published checks and candidates</h2>
    <dl class="stages">
      <div><dt><span class="badge st-draft">Draft</span></dt>
        <dd>The analysis is complete but not yet final. Where the check finds the claim Not substantiated, Misleading
            or Contradicted, the body concerned has not yet had the chance to reply; a check that finds a claim supported
            needs no reply. The verdict may change after a response or new evidence. Drafts are labelled as such on every
            page.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="badge st-published">Published</span></dt>
        <dd>The check is final. Where a right of reply applied, that step is complete and any response is published
            alongside the check.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><span class="badge st-candidate">Not yet checked</span></dt>
        <dd>A public statement has been recorded as a candidate. No assessment has been made, and listing it implies
            nothing about whether it is accurate.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-data">
    <h2 id="h-data">What the numbers, links and map show</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Counts reflect selection, not accuracy.</strong> The number of claims about a body, party or topic
          shows which statements were chosen for checking. The set is not a representative sample of anyone's
          statements, and it does not measure how often anyone is right.</li>
      <li><strong>Links between claims are leads, not accusations.</strong> A line on the map marks a shared
          mechanism, driver or pattern worth examining. It does not claim that one statement caused another. Pattern
          tags describe ways a statement can mislead; they are provisional until finished reports confirm them.</li>
      <li><strong>Places are approximate.</strong> A claim is pinned to the site it concerns, or, for national claims,
          to the institution that made it.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-sources">
    <h2 id="h-sources">Sources and archives</h2>
    <p>Each check lists its sources. Because pages change or disappear, Miżien keeps archived copies where it can, and
       each claim page says when a source has not yet been archived. Quoted statements, articles and papers remain the
       property of their authors and publishers.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-made">
    <h2 id="h-made">How checks are made</h2>
    <p>Research, calculations and drafting are assisted by AI tools. Every check is reviewed by a human maintainer,
       who decides what is published and is accountable for it. The method is set out in
       <a href="/methodology/how-a-verdict-is-reached/">How a verdict is reached</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-errors">
    <h2 id="h-errors">Errors and disagreement</h2>
    <p>Errors are corrected openly: each check carries a version number and a change log, and a correction that
       changes a verdict, a figure or an attribution is recorded with its date. To contest a check, use the
       <a href="{{ site.repo }}/issues/new?template=contest-verdict.yml">Contest a verdict</a> form and include the
       evidence. See the <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">corrections policy</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="h-reuse">
    <h2 id="h-reuse">Reuse</h2>
    <p>Reports, data and methodology are licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
       When citing a check, give its ID, version and date. The underlying data and code are in the
       <a href="{{ site.repo }}">public repository</a>.</p>
  </section>
</article>
